From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Wed, 4 May 1994 08:54:50 -0400
Subject: [#94-5-41] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage

 Hans Moravec was criticized at Extro 1 for ignoring the principle
of comparative advantage in his science fiction story of a time when
robotic slaves would eliminate all human jobs.
 There were many problems with his story (such as his hope that robots
would remain slaves to inferior beings for a non-trivial amount of time
(debated at length here last summer) and his advice to use the social
security system with the retirement age declining to zero to handle
the distribution of wealth, as opposed to just giving away anything
produced by robots as in James Hogan's _Voyage from Yesteryear_).
But his belief that the value of most human labor would go to zero
seems very reasonable to me.
 Comparative advantage is a good argument against protectionist barriers
between regions whose productivity differs by a few orders of magnitude
at most, but fails when applied to really different regions.
 Suppose that in the era of high robot productivity, interest rates have
risen to 10%/hour (hardly an unusual assumption if most economic processes
have been speeded up by a few orders of magnitude). Now imagine that a
robotic factory can transform $1.00 worth of rock into $1.10 worth of
widgets in 0.99999 hours (eeking out a tiny profit), but that replacing
any part of the manufacturing process with human labor would slow down
the process so that it took more than 1 hour produce the $1.10 in widgets.
If this phenomenon is sufficiently widespread, then nobody in his right mind
would invest in anything that uses a human to do anything that a robot could.
There would still be room for trade of services between humans, but this
would not enable trade between the human part of the economy and the robotic
part.


 I would like to thank the organizers of Extro 1 for an excellent weekend.
My initial reaction to reading that a conference was being planned was
to doubt that the movement was big enough or organized enough to support
a good one, but it was definitely worth traveling a great distance to
attend.
