From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Wed, 4 May 1994 10:03:32 -0700
Subject: [#94-5-43] Social Engineering

rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu writes:
>The problem I see with most social engineering is that it ultimately
>relies on the human agent, and since any model trivializes the
>complexity of that agent, there is bound to be a lag in the system.
>(and if that system is imposed by law, the human ends up being
>hurt when his personality and lifestyle doesn't fit the model)

Models in material science, computer science, biology, geology, you
name it, also leave out much of the complexity of what they model.
That's just the nature of modeling complex things.

>There are useful concepts to be learned, but I find it abhorrent
>to try to impose an engineered system on people (which is the way
>many social engineers *test* their system) Macroeconomics being
>a good example of a bad science wihch is used in bad ways and often
>ends up hurting a lot of people.
>   I'm not accusing Robin of wanting to do any of this, but let's
>remember where many "social engineers" end up -- government.

I would find it abhorrent if auto engineers forced me to drive their
new prototype cars, or some particular final design (as they did in
East Germany, I think).  But I have no problem with them hiring folks
to test their cars, nor with them trying to sell me cars they have
designed, and advertizing how well their tests went.

Similarly, the problem with state social engineers is the use of
force, not the concept of engineering.  For example, trying to force
everyone in the country to have the same health care arrangement,
because some central power has decided it is good for
them, is a really bad idea.  But there is nothing wrong with a private
group trying to work out a better institution for buying health care,
running some trial experiments to see how it works, and then trying to
persuade folks to volentarily sign up for their new plan.  

If there really isn't enough known about such social systems to allow
them to add much value, such attempts will fail in the marketplace.
There is no need to reject them a priori (I'm not accusing Ray of
doing this btw).  But I personally think we know enough for such
attempts to be profitable, on average, and will bet my career on that.

Robin Hanson



