By the way, I recently saw a talk by Pattie Maes about the
work of her group at the MIT Media Lab.  They're building
adaptive filters (they call them agents) for meeting
scheduling, mail sorting and netnews article picking.
The things try to learn from your own choices but also
different people's "agents" can trade hints with each
other.

These things work on either preselected characteristics of
situations (in the meeting and mail agents) or keywords
(in the news agent).  The meeting and mail agents are cool
in that they use "example-based reasoning", which means they
store your 500 or so most recent choices, find the five
that are "closest" to the current situation, and try to
decide from them.  The news agent uses some "genetic algorithm"
stuff (I hate how they call a certain algorithm "the" 
genetic algorithm).

