From: Brian Rauchfuss - PCD <brauchfu@pcocd2.intel.com>
Date: Wed, 4 May 94 11:49:11 PDT
Subject: [#94-5-46] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage

>  Suppose that in the era of high robot productivity, interest rates have
> risen to 10%/hour (hardly an unusual assumption if most economic processes
> have been speeded up by a few orders of magnitude). Now imagine that a

This seems like an unusual assumption to me, since interest rates did nothing
of the sort during the industrial revolution, the last time economic processes
increased by a few orders of magnitude.  Interest rates increased, but only
by a factor of 2 to 3.  Real interest rates moved by even less, since inflation
accounts for part of the apparent move.  (See the _Wall Street Waltz_ by Fisher
for a variety of interesting historical data).

Interest rates of 10%/hour (885%/day, or 10^365/year) implies that people are
willing to borrow at these rates, implying that most people are making profits
to make this worthwhile, implying that the economy is growing at about this
magnitude.  As long as entities such as humans and robots dominate the economy,
I don't think it will grow by a factor of 10^365 per year!  The growth of the
economy is based on increases in productivity (not the absolute value) and the
number of producers/consumers.  The number of producers/consumers is limited
by physical space (and CPU resources) and the productivity gains are one-time.

